{
  "mn33:0.1": "Middle Discourses 33 ",
  "mn33:0.2": "The Longer Discourse on the Cowherd ",
  "mn33:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "mn33:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "mn33:1.3": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, ",
  "mn33:1.4": "“Mendicants!” ",
  "mn33:1.5": "“Venerable sir,” they replied. ",
  "mn33:1.6": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "mn33:2.1": "“Mendicants, a cowherd with eleven factors can’t maintain and expand a herd of cattle. ",
  "mn33:2.2": "What eleven? ",
  "mn33:2.3": "It’s when a cowherd doesn’t know form, is unskilled in characteristics, doesn’t pick out flies’ eggs, doesn’t dress wounds, doesn’t spread smoke, doesn’t know the ford, doesn’t know satisfaction, doesn’t know the trail, is not skilled in ranges, milks dry, and doesn’t show extra respect to the bulls who are fathers and captains of the herd. ",
  "mn33:2.4": "A cowherd with these eleven factors can’t maintain and expand a herd of cattle. ",
  "mn33:3.1": "In the same way, a mendicant with eleven qualities can’t achieve growth, improvement, or maturity in this teaching and training. ",
  "mn33:3.2": "What eleven? ",
  "mn33:3.3": "It’s when a mendicant doesn’t know form, is unskilled in characteristics, doesn’t pick out flies’ eggs, doesn’t dress wounds, doesn’t spread smoke, doesn’t know the ford, doesn’t know satisfaction, doesn’t know the trail, is not skilled in ranges, milks dry, a